%T A397853 11069000,50171742,229687478,1060950862,4940525498,23176671326,
%U A397853 109459923570,520168765510,2486034072570,11944179868182,57666787378090,279683474689506,1362217001529574
%N A397853 Number of simple Baxter permutations of length n.
%C A397853 Simple is meant in the sense of A111111: the only intervals mapped onto intervals are the singletons and the whole permutation. In particular 1, 12 and 21 count as simple, giving a(1) = 1 and a(2) = 2.
%C A397853 The Baxter class is closed under substitution, so every Baxter permutation is the inflation of a unique simple Baxter permutation by Baxter permutations. The formula below is the Albert-Atkinson substitution decomposition specialized to this class, and has the same shape as the g.f. of A111111.
%C A397853 Equivalently, a(n) is the number of prime mosaic floorplans with n rooms: dissections of a rectangle into n rectangles, taken up to R-equivalence, in which no set of at least 2 and at most n-1 rooms has rectangular union. Under the Ackerman-Barequet-Pinter bijection between mosaic floorplans and Baxter permutations, blocks of the floorplan correspond to intervals of the permutation, so "prime" corresponds to "simple".
%C A397853 This is the sequence p_n of Felsner, Fusy and Noy, the number of prime 2-orientations with n inner faces, where a quadrangulation is prime if it has at least 3 inner faces and every 4-cycle bounds a face. They print it from n = 5 as 2, 0, 12, 24, 116, 418, 1722, ...; a(n) = p_n for n >= 3, while p_1 = p_2 = 0. Their g.f. P satisfies Q(z) = z + 2*Q(z)^2/(1+Q(z)) + P(Q(z)) (their Lemma 2), where Q is the Baxter g.f.
%C A397853 Felsner, Fusy and Noy also observe that p_n counts bipolar orientations on rooted 3-connected planar maps with n edges, by specializing the bijection between 2-orientations and bipolar orientations to prime quadrangulations.
%C A397853 a(n) = 0 exactly for n in {3, 4, 6}, and a(n) > 0 for every n >= 7.
%C A397853 Positivity beyond 6 follows from the construction of Balachandran and Koroth: if pi is a simple Baxter permutation of length n whose last two entries are n, 2, then (n+1), pi_1, ..., pi_{n-1}, (n+2), 2 is again simple and Baxter and ends in n+2, 2; iterating from 41352 gives every odd n >= 5 and from 75146382 every even n >= 8.
%C A397853 Verified by direct enumeration of all Baxter permutations of length n for n <= 14, and independently for n <= 20 against the bipolar-orientation route of the previous comment, summing 4*beta(G)*E/|Aut(G)| over the 3-connected planar maps G with E = n+1 edges.
%H A397853 Meghanto Majumder, <a href="/A397853/b397853.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..500</a>
%H A397853 E. Ackerman, G. Barequet and R. Y. Pinter, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2006.03.018">A bijection between permutations and floorplans, and its applications</a>, Discrete Appl. Math. 154 (2006), 1674-1684.
%H A397853 M. H. Albert and M. D. Atkinson, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2005.06.016">Simple permutations and pattern restricted permutations</a>, Discrete Math. 300 (2005), 1-15.
%H A397853 S. Balachandran and S. Koroth, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.1374">Sub-families of Baxter permutations based on pattern avoidance</a>, arXiv:1112.1374 [cs.DM], 2011-2016. See Theorem 6.
%H A397853 F. R. K. Chung, R. L. Graham, V. E. Hoggatt Jr. and M. Kleiman, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-3165(78)90068-7">The number of Baxter permutations</a>, J. Combin. Theory Ser. A 24 (1978), 382-394.
%H A397853 S. Felsner, E. Fusy and M. Noy, <a href="https://doi.org/10.46298/dmtcs.505">Asymptotic enumeration of orientations</a>, Discrete Math. Theor. Comput. Sci. 12 (2010), no. 2, 249-262. The sequence p_n is printed on p. 250; Lemma 2 (p. 255) gives the functional equation below, Lemmas 3-5 the singular analysis, and Theorem 2 (p. 258) the asymptotics.
%F A397853 G.f.: A(x) = 2*x/(1+x) + 2*x^2 - G(x), where G is the compositional inverse (series reversion) of B(x) = Sum_{n>=1} A001181(n)*x^n. Equivalently B(x) = x + 2*B(x)^2/(1+B(x)) + A(B(x)).
%F A397853 a(n) = 2*(-1)^(n-1) + 2*[n=2] - g(n), where g(n) = [x^n] G(x). Compare the corresponding formula on A111111.
%F A397853 By Lagrange inversion, g(n) = (1/n)*[u^(n-1)] (u/B(u))^n.
%F A397853 G satisfies the second-order nonlinear differential equation L(G) = 0, where L(Y) = -(Y^2 - 7*Y^3 - 8*Y^4)*Y'' + (6*Y - 28*Y^2 - 16*Y^3)*(Y')^2 + (6*x - 12*x*Y - 12*Y)*(Y')^3. This is the linear differential equation satisfied by the Baxter g.f., transported through the inversion.
%F A397853 Exact nonlinear recurrence: writing G_{<n}(x) = x + Sum_{k=2..n-1} g(k)*x^k, one has g(n) = [x^n] L(G_{<n}) / ((n+2)*(n+3)) for n >= 2, because the coefficient of g(n) in [x^n] L(G) is exactly -(n+2)*(n+3). Every other contribution is a Cauchy convolution of all earlier coefficients, so this is a recurrence of unbounded width, not a P-recurrence.
%F A397853 a(n) ~ c * n^(-4) * gamma^n, where rho = B(1/8) = 0.1811735685..., gamma = 1/rho = 5.5195689315..., q = B'(1/8) and c = 2^14*rho^3/(q^4*sqrt(3)*Pi) = 0.5097001... (Felsner, Fusy and Noy, Theorem 2, where the constant is written c = Q(1/8)^3*2^14/(Q'(1/8)^4*sqrt(3)*Pi) and gamma = Q(1/8)^(-1)).
%F A397853 a(n)/A001181(n) ~ (512*rho^3/q^4)*(gamma/8)^n with gamma/8 = 0.6899461164..., so prime objects are an exponentially vanishing fraction of all Baxter objects.
%F A397853 From _Vaclav Kotesovec_, Aug 10 2026: (Start)
%F A397853 More precise values of the constants listed above:
%F A397853 B(1/8) = 0.1811735685178471157410872726002332236392466265323968341105100638104...
%F A397853 gamma = 5.51956893149947319406373283469436730276223730088886394825535089752198...
%F A397853 B'(1/8) = 2.434556237047779481475936971729601755105387308814983987565172312366...
%F A397853 c = 0.509700090867291515969433793047006286260658765633872424197845096246497248... (End)
%e A397853 There are no simple Baxter permutations of length 3, 4 or 6.
%e A397853 The a(5) = 2 permutations of length 5 are 25314 and 41352; as mosaic floorplans these are the two pinwheels.
%e A397853 The a(7) = 12 permutations of length 7 are 2475316, 2631574, 2736415, 2753146, 3742516, 4137526, 4751362, 5146372, 6135742, 6152473, 6257314 and 6413572.
%o A397853 (PARI) seq(N) = {my(b=vector(N)); b[1]=1; b[2]=2; for(n=3, N, b[n] = ((7*n^2+7*n-2)*b[n-1] + 8*(n-1)*(n-2)*b[n-2])/((n+2)*(n+3))); Vec(2 + 2*x^2 - 2/(1+x) + O(x^(N+1)) - serreverse(x*Ser(b, x, N)))};
